  <section aria-labelledby="how-i-work">
    <h2 id="how-i-work">How I work</h2>
    <p>I enjoy product problems where the technology is promising but the path to value is still unclear. I try to connect four things:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>a customer problem</li>
      <li>the capability and limits of the technology</li>
      <li>a workflow people will adopt</li>
      <li>a commercial reason for the product to exist</li>
    </ol>
    <p>That has meant working across discovery, strategy, roadmaps, UX, APIs, data and model requirements, evaluation, delivery, go-to-market and enterprise adoption. I’m comfortable moving between conversations with customers, designers, engineers, data scientists, sales teams and company leadership.</p>
    <p>In practice, especially in the last few years, this has meant searching wide for problem surfaces, going deep into promising workflows that were previously difficult to automate, assessing ROI and consistently testing the edge of frontier-model capabilities while working within the resource constraints of startups.</p>
    <p>My AI product experience predates the current GenAI wave. It began with NLP and traditional machine learning, expanded into computer vision and human-in-the-loop systems and now includes Graph RAG, LLM and agentic workflows. I care less about attaching an AI label to a product and a lot more about whether the system is reliable, useful and worth adopting.</p>
  </section>

